specification:
  examCode: Cambridge IGCSE Computer Science 0478 (2026-2028)
  unitSummary: >-
    Everything a computer processes—from your favourite song to the code running this website—boils
    down to ones and zeros. In this unit, we peel back the layers to understand how computers
    translate our complex world (numbers, text, sound, and images) into simple binary states.
  objectives:
    - 'Number Systems: How to navigate Binary (Base-2), Denary (Base-10), and Hexadecimal (Base-16).'
    - >-
      Binary Arithmetic: The rules for adding binary numbers, performing logical shifts, and Two's
      Complement.
    - 'Multimedia Representation: Text, Sound, and Images.'
    - 'Data Storage: Measurement units and compression.'
  outcomes:
    - >-
      Convert Fluidly: Switch numbers between binary, denary, and hexadecimal formats without a
      calculator.
    - >-
      Calculate Accurately: Perform binary addition (handling overflows) and logical shifts.
      Calculate file sizes for images and sound. Crucial: You must use 1024 (not 1000) when
      converting between units like KiB, MiB, and GiB.
    - >-
      Explain the "Why": Justify why hexadecimal is used (e.g., for MAC addresses or error codes)
      and why specific compression methods are chosen for different file types.
    - >-
      Analyse Trade-offs: Describe how changing the resolution, colour depth, or sample rate affects
      both the quality of the media and its file size.
  subtopics:
    - 1.1 Number Representation
    - '1.2 Text, Sound & Images'
    - 1.3 Data Storage & Compression
